hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well  in book 1 of on duties the work of stoic  ethics that he lays up Cicero discusses  the virtues and in particular the virtue  of justice one of the subdivisions of  the virtue of justice is what he terms  beneficence and in other places  benevolence this is a disposition of  doing good to other people going beyond  the rules or what's required to do more  than that to help them out to show  generosity now one of the questions that  we can ask is how much under what  conditions and and one of the possible  answers that that some people give is  well you should always give as much as  you possibly can you should always do  you should always help as much as you  possibly can that the more that you do  have that the better a person you are  Cicero doesn't take that position he  does think that we ought to go beyond  just what you know people's rights are  or what the law says or what we've  promised we should do more than that but  we shouldn't go too far and so this  raises a very interesting question why  do we need guidelines about how far we  want to go or who we ought to be  generous orbán if assent with why  shouldn't we just simply say whenever  you see a person in need help them out  whenever anybody could be assisted in  some way by your talents or your money  or your social position use those to  help those people well you know one of  the problems that comes up is we only  have so many resources and some of those  resources are almost impossible to add  to and then we can get more wealth and  we can certainly develop more talents  but you cannot get yourself more time  and although you can train yourself to  pay more attention you can only pay  attention to so many people at any given  time are you depriving others by giving  to those  who you do help are you committing  injustice in that way sister would say  no you're actually being prudent and  you're being just by by doing so we need  guidelines to help us out in these sort  of situations particularly the people  who are most inclined to help other  people out need these guidelines the  most the people who are kind of  standoffish and and stingy and and not  inclined to help people well they they  might need a little reminder - you know  you should do some charity work or some  volunteering or give of yourself help  your neighbor out but the people who are  always giving might go too far so if  this role provides some very important  guidelines here the first up with it  which well let's run through them in  order so they are don't harm the person  or other persons this is the first one  he says the second one is don't go  beyond your means don't go beyond what  you actually have or what you can  sustain proportion your benevolence or  beneficence to the merit of the person  and this actually covers quite a lot as  we'll see show proper gratitude that  that's an obligation as well and then  exercise judgment and consideration when  you are giving don't just give because  it makes you feel good give because it's  actually the right thing to do in this  particular case so let's look at each of  these in turn  he says there's many who transgress this  first one don't harm the person or other  persons because what they do is they  take from one person to give to another  he uses as an example some of the people  in Roman society who are robbing one  person to enrich another they expect to  be thought generous to their friends if  they put them in the way of getting rich  no matter by what means Cicero says this  is beyond the pale this is so remote  from moral duty nothing can be more  opposed to duty so he talks about Sola  and he also talks about Caesar Gaius  sees  how they took things from their rightful  owners to give them to other people  they're not really being generous by  doing that they're being unjust this is  not a form of benevolence even though  those who are benefiting from it are  probably inclined to look at it that way  and to talk about it that way  but if you're harming other people in  the process you're not really being  benevolent you're not being good to  people in general we might also think  about cases where you could in fact be  harming the person with the attention  with the money with the resources with  whatever it is that you're providing  them with for instance if if we think  that we know better than them what it is  that they really need or desire and we  don't take account of that we give them  gifts that are that are unwelcome those  aren't really gifts those are really  obstacles or you might say irritations  what about other cases you know should  we give an alcoholic a drink at the  Christmas party no you shouldn't do that  right should you enable people in other  ways that's not really being good to  them it's not being generous to them so  it's important not to harm other people  by the ways in which we're giving he  also talks about not going beyond our  means this is a very important thing as  well he says those who wish to be more  open handed than circumstances allow of  are guilty of two main faults they're  doing wrong to somebody else they're not  helping they're harming somebody namely  those who are closest to them their  family their friends those who are  dependent about them people who can't  stop giving gifts to random strangers  are going to exhaust their means and not  be able to take care of those people who  they ought to be most closely concerned  with we might say similar things about  the  who have nothing to give to their  community because they've given it all  to some other far distant cause so  there's that says such generosity too  often engenders a passion for plundering  and misappropriating property in order  to supply the means for making large  gifts he says a third thing as well we  may observe that many people do many  things that seem to be inspired more  from a spirit he says of ostentation  than by her heartfelt kindness so there  are some people who want to appear to be  big shots and they go beyond their means  if we do this this is not good for us  and ultimately it's not good for those  who were giving - because you know if  they really do deserve it wouldn't it be  better if we do it in a sustainable way  and this could go not just for money  this could go for other resources like  time you know if you're committing too  much so that you're not actually getting  enough sleep that's a problem  you do need to sleep in order to  function well if you're giving so much  of your we might say emotional energy  that you have none left for yourself  sooner or later you will collapse and  you're not going to be helping anybody  else when they have to take care of you  so that's a real issue this is an  important guideline the third one is  quite complex and we're only going to go  into some of the things that that Cicero  says here about proportioning the the  the beneficence or benevolence - the  merit of the person he says an acts of  kindness we want to weigh with  discrimination the worthiness the value  of the object of our benevolence we  should take into consideration the moral  character of the person we should be  beneficent towards those who are  actually honest rather than to those who  are dishonest we should be beneficent  towards those who themselves will show  generosity or show clemency or mercy to  other people rather than to those who  are hard-hearted irritable antagonistic  this is what Cicero would say it doesn't  mean that we can't show any to those who  are much more disagreeable people who  make other people miserable but it means  that the people who aren't making other  people miserable they should probably  get the first shot at what it is that we  have to give so there there's one aspect  of that he also says we should take into  consideration the attitude of the person  towards us the intimacy of their  relations to us and our common social  ties says all of these you know would be  nice to combine the into one person but  often that's not the case so we may have  somebody who's closer to us by you know  virtue of being our cousin but they're  not a particularly good person and then  we may have a person who's really great  but they're not as close to us which one  should we begin to we want to proportion  it in some way to that he also has a  very important reminder here for us he  says we don't want to set the standard  so high that nobody can meet them he  says there's a beautiful line the people  we live with are not perfect and ideally  wise but we would do very well if there  were found in that Buffy as he says  semblance of virtue the simulacra of  their touches so he says nobody's to be  entirely neglected who shows any trace  of virtue we don't have to have people  who are perfectly virtuous for us to be  you know doing the right thing and  helping them out if we see a tendency  towards it let's help them if we can and  those who are more willing to do that  those people do deserve our beneficence  our benevolence more than those who say  screw it I'm not going to have anything  to do with that one of the other things  that he 